{"author":{"id":"89da5fec8eff6d69dbcdd68f81be7e9163b16f34b17e978ecf3554487379f453"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1774128396,"nonce":"8tgqHSVvJ1tPV4tABr9vrtxJeb4=","message":"**Distribution Analysis — Script Coupling \u0026 Packaging Strategy**\n\n## Script Coupling Assessment\n\n| Script | Lines | Mandible API calls | git-bug dep | Coupling |\n|--------|-------|-------------------|-------------|----------|\n| agent-commit | 194 | 2 (session link, work-unit activate) — non-fatal | Yes (title lookup) | MEDIUM |\n| wt | 362 | 1 (work-unit claim via --issue) — non-fatal | No | LOW |\n| dwt | 177 | 1 (work-unit release) — non-fatal | No | LOW |\n| wt-merge | 235 | 2 (work-unit query + resolve) — non-fatal | No | LOW |\n| wt-sync | 89 | 0 | No | NONE |\n| wt-move | 85 | 0 (indirect via wt) | No | NONE |\n| mandible shim | 17 | N/A — IS Mandible | N/A | 100% |\n\nKey finding: ALL Mandible API calls in wt* and agent-commit are non-fatal (wrapped in `|| true` or `|| { warning }`) . These scripts function perfectly without Mandible running. The coupling is purely optional enhancement — work-unit tracking layered on top of otherwise generic git tooling.\n\n## Mandible Repo Already Has\n\n- `bin/mandible` — full CLI dispatcher (59 lines), routes to cli/*.sh\n- `cli/` — 7 subcommand scripts (auth-merge 26k, auth-request 18k, inbox, issue, search, task, wrap)\n- The dotfiles `mandible` shim (17 lines) just exec's `bin/mandible` from the repo\n- Existing issue d0a2ff4: auth-merge.sh has hardcoded `../../../../bin/dwt` path — proof that cli/ already depends on wt* scripts\n\n## Recommended Strategy: Option A — Everything in mandible repo\n\n### Structure\n```\nmandible/\n  bin/\n    mandible          # existing dispatcher (add wt, agent-commit routes)\n    agent-commit      # standalone entry point (symlink or wrapper)\n    wt                # standalone entry point\n    dwt               # standalone entry point (or wt delete)\n    wt-merge          # standalone entry point (or wt merge)\n    wt-sync           # standalone entry point (or wt sync)\n    wt-move           # standalone entry point (or wt move)\n  cli/\n    auth-merge.sh     # existing\n    auth-request.sh   # existing\n    ...\n```\n\n### Why not split into two packages (agent-tools + mandible)?\n1. **Single user project.** Two-package overhead buys nothing.\n2. **The wt* scripts already have Mandible integration points** — splitting forces either duplicating the integration or making agent-tools depend on mandible anyway.\n3. **auth-merge.sh already calls dwt** (d0a2ff4). The dependency graph is already bidirectional.\n4. **Tauri bundling is simpler with one repo** — everything ships in the sidecar.\n\n### Why not keep in dotfiles?\n1. **Blocks Tauri Level 2 distribution** — new users would need dotfiles just for worktree management.\n2. **Version coupling is implicit** — a daemon update might break a script that was last touched 3 months ago in dotfiles.\n3. **Already causing pain** — d0a2ff4 (hardcoded paths) exists because the scripts and cli/ are in different repos.\n\n### Unify wt* into subcommands?\n**Recommendation: keep them separate, add dispatch aliases.** The `mandible` dispatcher can route `mandible wt create`, `mandible wt delete`, etc., but the standalone scripts (wt, dwt, wt-merge, wt-sync, wt-move) should remain as-is in bin/. Reasons:\n- Muscle memory: `wt feature/foo` is faster than `mandible wt create feature/foo`\n- Composability: scripts call each other (wt-merge calls dwt, wt-move calls wt)\n- Agent CLAUDE.md references: changing `dwt` to `wt delete` means updating CLAUDE.md in every repo + the global CLAUDE.md\n\nDotfiles would then just symlink mandible/bin/* into PATH (or add mandible/bin/ to PATH).\n\n### Agent key question\nKeep key config in shell environment (GIT_CONFIG_COUNT pattern). agent-commit should NOT manage keys. Optionally add `mandible doctor` to verify environment setup — but this is low priority for a single-user system.\n\n### Migration path\n1. Copy scripts from dotfiles/bin/ to mandible/bin/\n2. Update dotfiles/bin/ entries to be thin shims pointing to mandible/bin/ (or just symlinks)\n3. Fix d0a2ff4 (auth-merge.sh hardcoded dwt path) — trivial once dwt is in the same repo\n4. Update mandible/bin/mandible dispatcher to route wt and agent-commit subcommands\n5. Later: Tauri bundles mandible/bin/ and mandible/cli/ as sidecar\n\n## Gemini Analysis (independent, for comparison)\nGemini recommended the same core strategy (everything in mandible repo) with one difference: it suggested unifying wt* into `wt create/delete/merge/sync/move` subcommands under a master `mandible wt` namespace. The standalone-scripts-in-bin/ approach above is preferred for backward compatibility, but the dispatcher-alias approach gives both options.","files":null}]}